From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AltarsCN <337293323+Altars3668@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 29 Sep 2026 14:13:05 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] windowscodecs: Write GIF metadata: block writers, the GIF
 metadata writers and SetMetadataByName().

PowerPoint's animated GIF export asks the GIF encoder for its metadata
query writer, got E_NOTIMPL and gave up without a frame, and from then
on rejected every automation call until it was killed.  Behind that
stub the whole chain was missing: SetMetadataByName() returned S_OK for
every format and wrote nothing, the frame encoder's block writer was
E_NOTIMPL throughout, the five GIF blocks had readers only, and Commit
wrote no extension.

The encoder and each frame now keep a list of metadata writers behind
IWICMetadataBlockWriter; the encoder's query writer is built on its
list.  SetMetadataByName() sets the value on the writer that holds the
item and, when a block on the path does not exist yet, creates a writer
for its format and adds it to the parent.  There are writers for the
logical screen and image descriptors, the graphic control, application
and comment extensions, with the items and types the readers produce.
Commit writes the descriptors' fields that were set, the file's
application and comment extensions after the global colour table, and
each frame's graphic control and comment extensions before its image.
PowerPoint now writes a two-frame, looping GIF with the slide delays in
two seconds and keeps answering; its sweep goes from 45 to 48 steps.

The reference Windows machine was unreachable, so these are not
measured yet (tools/gifencprobe will be run there): that new block
lists start empty, that a frame without /grctlext gets no graphic
control extension, the errors for a value of the wrong type, an unknown
item and a block a GIF cannot hold, that the encoder answers QI for the
block writer, and how a trailing zero in /appext/Data is written.
Interlacing is refused, as the LZW writer only writes rows in order.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>

